        "synthesis": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "synthesis",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Research Prim_ST overhead: use cycle-check removal as a control and investigate avoiding per-call graph/matrix copies or scratch allocations, without changing include/exclude behavior or vertex-selection order. Preserve exact tie behavior and output; do not repeat the slower flat-matrix conversion without new evidence.",
            "B": "Focus on find_min_key: measure a simple sequential scan that avoids copying its inputs (for example, const references) while preserving signed-key ordering, first-index ties, and disconnected detection. The packed reduction was only marginally faster than baseline and the SIMD/byte-flag variant was slower; do not assume parallel reduction helps on this 4-core ARM64 target.",
            "C": "Investigate reducing dense graph setup and traversal work. The resize-plus-row-initialization candidate was fastest at 0.005211 s versus the 0.005363 s baseline; validate its benefit across general input sizes, and explore edge-oriented adjacency to avoid dense initialization/scans. Re-measure traversal parallelism and thresholds rather than tuning to the measured cases; preserve valid-input behavior and output ordering.",
            "G": "Integrate only candidates with reviewed semantics and correctness evidence. Start by measuring the row-initialization and cycle-check-removal candidates individually, then test combinations: their gains are not additive by assumption. Use the existing correctness and measurement process unchanged, preserve output/tie behavior, and retain the fastest valid integration; do not modify tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source beyond integration.",
            "analysis": "The official problem is tropical paths in an undirected colored graph; the supplied source uses a dense adjacency matrix, repeated Prim-style spanning-tree work, and path traversal. On the 4-core ARM64 target, the baseline mean was 0.005363 s (correct on 4 cases). Individual reported means were 0.005211 s for parallel row initialization, 0.005236 s for removing the redundant cycle check, 0.005304 s for a packed reduction, and 0.005309 s for traversal changes. Contiguous-matrix/const-reference and SIMD/byte-flag candidates were slower at about 0.00551 s. These are small absolute differences from a short workload, so treat them as leads, not guarantees; correctness must remain primary."
          },
