            "C": "Research a low-overhead, robust sequential alternative such as introsort: insertion sort for small ranges, a worst-case heapsort fallback, and a partition strategy that handles duplicates. Replace only sorting behavior; retain exact generated values, sortedness validation, and MD5 output. Compare full-run performance against the existing quicksort.",
            "G": "Individually validate candidate programs, then measure any proposed integration on the actual runner and official cases, including the required repeated timing samples. Confirm exact output/checksum correctness. Compare integrated end-to-end results with the baseline and each candidate; do not assume gains add, and reject combinations that measure worse. Keep generation and checksum unchanged.",
            "analysis": "The supplied baseline is valid: mean 0.068926 seconds (samples 0.068874–0.068967), score 0.5, and correctness on 4 cases. The target is ARM64 with 4 CPU cores and no GPU. The source serially generates data, uses a shared custom PRNG for recursive two-way quicksort pivots, then checks sortedness and computes MD5. No phase timings or case sizes are supplied, so do not assume sorting dominates; the short runtime makes parallel overhead a particular risk. Use generic runtime heuristics, never special-case known inputs, and preserve correctness for every valid input."
