          "plan": {
            "A": "Research a correct parallel bucket pipeline for the 4-core ARM64 target. Count entries per bucket, compute prefix offsets, and partition into disjoint ranges so skewed buckets cannot overrun the current equal-sized slices. Then parallelize independent bucket work where useful, preserving ascending bucket order and the existing strcmp-based ordering within each bucket. Measure the whole pipeline, not just its parallel section.",
            "B": "Investigate replacing the insertion sort in sort() with a general comparison sort for each bucket. Keep the current lexicographic strcmp semantics and sort indices rather than moving input strings; handle duplicates and arbitrarily large buckets correctly. Compare options, including whether a small-bucket insertion-sort threshold helps, and benchmark under both balanced and skewed distributions without tailoring to particular tests.",
            "C": "Investigate reducing serial input/output overhead: the source uses fscanf for every string and printf for every output line. Consider buffered parsing and buffered output while preserving whitespace handling, token contents, and exactly one sorted string per line. Keep storage and parsing safe for the declared sizes and do not assume fixed test data.",
            "G": "Integrate candidate improvements only after checking each for correctness and measuring it. The baseline is correct on 4 cases at a mean 0.414851 s (score 0.5); the target has 4 CPU cores and no GPU. Benchmark each candidate and the combined program against the same evaluation setup: gains may interact or regress when combined. Preserve the source's output format and lexicographic ordering semantics, and reject any optimization that fails correctness or does not improve measured runtime.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source groups strings by their first byte and insertion-sorts indices within each bucket using strcmp, making large buckets potentially quadratic. It assigns each bucket a fixed size/nbuckets slice of one index array, which is unsafe if bucket sizes are uneven; exact counts and prefix offsets address that. Per-token fscanf/printf may also contribute overhead. The only run measurement provided is a correct baseline on four cases at 0.414851 s, score 0.5; no candidate measurements are available, so the proposed ideas need independent and combined measurement."
